---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    route={['Spain', 'Andalucía', 'Seville']}
    label="Guide · Sixteen Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in Seville:"
    titleEmphasis="Sixteen, Grouped and Judged"
    standfirst="Not a ranked list of interchangeable attractions. Sixteen experiences, grouped by where they sit and what they are for, each with the time it takes, what it pairs with, and an honest answer to whether you can skip it."
    stats={[
      { num: '16', label: 'Entries' },
      { num: '6', label: 'Groups' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Need Booking' },
      { num: '6', label: 'Free or Nearly' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          The trouble with a list of things to do in Seville is that the list is
          short and the city is not. Six or seven buildings carry the reputation,
          they stand within a few hundred metres of one another, and any account
          of them will name the same six or seven. The genuinely useful work
          begins one layer down, among the fifteen or twenty things that are
          worth a stranger's afternoon and are not obligatory — and there the
          question is no longer <em>what is there</em> but <em>which of these,
          and instead of what</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this is a list with verdicts in it. Sixteen entries, grouped into
          six sections by what they actually are rather than by rank, and each one
          answered on the same five points: why it matters, what makes it
          specifically Seville rather than generically Spanish, how long it takes,
          what it sits next to, and who can reasonably leave it out. Several
          entries carry an explicit permission to skip. Two are recommended over
          near-identical alternatives. Sixteen is not a round number and is not
          meant to be — it is the count that survived the test of whether
          something specific could be said about why each one deserves your time.
        </p>
        <p>
          What this page deliberately does not do is put them in order. Sequencing
          Seville is a separate and quite difficult problem, solved elsewhere on
          this site for <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">three days</a> and for
          <a href="/one-day-in-seville/">a single one</a>. Here the geography
          appears only as pairing advice, so you can assemble your own days out of
          it. Everything below has been visited: the sixteen are drawn from a
          nine-night stay recorded as
          <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten days using Seville as a base</a>.
          The opening hours, prices and booking rules are current research rather
          than memory, and are dated accordingly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/pabellon-mudejar-reflected.jpg`}
      alt="The Pabellón Mudéjar in Parque de María Luisa, Seville, mirrored in its still lily-padded pool: a brick and tile pavilion with two towers and a green-tiled roof, flanked by two tall ornate iron lamp standards, with a small fountain playing in the water."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The Mudéjar Pavilion in Parque de María Luisa, built for the 1929 Exposition and now the Museum of Arts and Popular Customs. It is a ten-minute walk from the most photographed square in Seville and almost nobody comes round the back to look at it."
      priority
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="how-to-use" rule={false}>How to read the entries</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Each one closes with a single line of mechanics: <strong>Time</strong>,
        <strong>Pairs with</strong>, <strong>Booking</strong>, and
        <strong>Skip if</strong> — the last being the part most lists leave out.
        A verdict of <em>first visit</em> means it belongs in a stay of three days
        or fewer; <em>longer stay</em> means it earns its place from about the
        fourth day onward. Prices and hours were verified on 29 July 2026 and are
        the fastest-moving thing on this page.
      </p>
    </div>

