  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="monumental"
      number="I."
      name="Monumental Seville"
      epithet="Four entries inside a radius of 250 metres, and the reason three days in the city is possible at all"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Around Plaza del Triunfo, Seville put a royal palace, the largest Gothic
        cathedral in the world, the paper archive of an empire and a quarter of
        medieval lanes within sight of one another. No other Spanish city
        concentrates so much in so little ground. It is also the group that fills
        first and gets hottest, which is why all four entries below carry a note
        about the hour.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="alcazar" level={3} rule={false}>1. Real Alcázar</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A palace lived in without interruption since the twelfth century, and the
        single best thing in Seville. What makes it more than beautiful is the
        decision recorded in its fabric: when the Christian kings inherited a
        Moorish palace they extended it in the Moorish manner rather than
        replacing it, and kept doing so for four hundred years. The Patio de las
        Doncellas and the gilded dome of the Salón de Embajadores are the set
        pieces; the walled gardens beyond them are close to half the visit and
        are the part visitors run out of time for. Nothing in Andalucía explains
        the region faster.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 2 to 2½ hours, half of it outdoors ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Cathedral, three minutes away ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> essential and timed; general admission €15.50,
        reduced €8.00, Cuarto Real Alto a further €5.50; open 09:30–19:00 April
        to September and 09:30–17:00 October to March; the early slots sell out
        weeks ahead · <strong>Skip if</strong> never. First visit.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="cathedral" level={3}>2. Catedral de Sevilla and the Giralda</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The largest Gothic church on earth by volume, built on the footprint of
        the mosque it replaced, with the mosque's minaret still standing beside
        it as a bell tower. Two things justify the ticket. The Retablo Mayor is a
        wall of gilded and polychrome carving assembled over most of a century
        and holding something over a thousand figures — an object genuinely
        difficult to take in at one standing. And the Giralda is climbed by
        thirty-five shallow ramps rather than stairs, built wide enough, the story
        goes, for a rider to ascend on horseback. There is no lift.
      </p>
      <p>
        A caution belonging to this year rather than the twelfth century:
        improvement works on the belfry have been under way through 2026, the
        visiting hours have moved more than once because of them, and no
        completion date has been published. The notices neither close the climb
        nor confirm that it is open.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 1½ hours including the tower ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Alcázar and the Archivo, both minutes
        away · <strong>Booking</strong> yes, timed and capacity-limited; €13.00
        online or €14.00 at the window, reduced €7.00 / €8.00, current from 1
        January 2026. The same ticket also admits you to the Church of El
        Salvador a few streets north. Check
        <a href="https://www.catedraldesevilla.es/en/cultural-visit/schedules-and-rates/" rel="noopener">the
        official schedule page</a> in the week before you travel ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> never, though the tower climb is genuinely
        optional. First visit.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="archivo" level={3}>3. Archivo General de Indias</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The most under-visited important building in Seville, and free. A
        Renaissance merchants' exchange, later given over to the entire written
        record of Spain's empire in the Americas: roughly eight kilometres of
        shelving, uniform boxes, the journals of the conquistadors, letters in
        Columbus's own hand, all in a vaulted upper gallery with a chequered
        marble floor cut by window light. Few rooms anywhere hold so much
        consequence so quietly. It is also air-conditioned, which in July makes it
        the most efficient forty minutes in the city.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 30 to 45 minutes · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the
        Cathedral, directly opposite · <strong>Booking</strong> none needed below
        twenty people; free; Tuesday to Saturday 09:30–17:00 with last entry
        16:30, Sunday and public holidays 10:00–14:00, <strong>closed
        Mondays</strong> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have a single day and it
        is a Monday. Otherwise no. First visit.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="santa-cruz" level={3}>4. Barrio de Santa Cruz</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The old Jewish quarter, immediately east of the Cathedral: whitewash,
        orange trees, courtyards behind iron gates, lanes narrow enough in places
        that two people cannot walk them abreast. There is no ticket, no route
        and nothing to queue for, which makes it the one entry on this page that
        cannot be done badly except by doing it at the wrong hour. At noon it is
        an oven with a crowd in it. At five it is one of the pleasantest places in
        Andalucía to be lost on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 45 minutes to 1½ hours, unstructured ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> everything in this group, and drifts of its
        own accord into an evening towards Calle Mateos Gago and Plaza de la
        Alfalfa · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Free ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> the ground is a problem — it is cobbled and
        uneven throughout. First visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>Four things, four hundred metres, and one instruction: not at midday.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="river"
      number="II."
      name="The River and the Trade"
      epithet="Why a city a hundred kilometres from open water grew rich on an ocean it could not see"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        For two centuries the whole of Spain's trade with the Americas was
        required by law to pass through this one river port. That fact built
        Seville, and the four entries here are what it left: a watchtower, a
        bullring, a tile industry and the water itself, running north–south along
        the Guadalquivir with one bridge in the middle.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="guadalquivir" level={3} rule={false}>5. The Guadalquivir and the Triana bank</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Not a sight, and the most under-rated thing on this page. The river
        divides Seville into the old city and Triana — sailors, potters, flamenco,
        a neighbourhood that knows it is not the centre and has never much wanted
        to be — and the walk along Calle Betis on the west bank, facing the lit
        old town across the current, is one of the genuinely fine hours available
        anywhere in Spain. At dawn and at dusk the water carries rowers and
        canoeists; the crews training on it are a working club rather than an
        attraction, which is exactly why watching them is worth ten minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour, or an evening · <strong>Pairs with</strong>
        the market and the ceramics museum in the morning, or nothing at all after
        dark · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Free ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day; the bridge is the specific
        thing that breaks a single-day route. First visit if you have two days or
        more.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/guadalquivir-at-sunset.jpg`}
      alt="The Guadalquivir at sunset seen from above: a lone kayaker and a group of four paddlers on violet water, the far bank in silhouette with white apartment buildings, trees, construction cranes and a lit modern building, beneath an orange and violet sky."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Eight in the evening, and the river is being used rather than admired — training crews, a kayak, and cranes on the far bank. Seville's working edges are part of the argument for walking the water rather than photographing it."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="torre-del-oro" level={3}>6. Torre del Oro and the Naval Museum</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        An Almohad watchtower of 1221 that has since been a prison, a chapel, a
        gunpowder store and a warehouse, and is now a small maritime museum with
        a viewing platform on top. It is the cheapest ticketed thing in central
        Seville and the most modest, and its real function is as a marker: it
        stands where the American cargoes were counted, and its dozen rooms of
        charts and models put the river trade into a form you can look at. Thirty
        to forty-five minutes, no more.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 30 to 45 minutes · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the
        Maestranza, five minutes north, and the riverbank walk ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none; admission is around €3 with a reduced rate
        of about half that, and entry has traditionally been free on Mondays.
        Published hours differ between sources, so confirm before making a
        journey for it · <strong>Skip if</strong> you are short of time — the
        exterior and the walk along the water stand perfectly well without the
        interior. Longer stay.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="maestranza" level={3}>7. Plaza de Toros de la Real Maestranza</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        One of the oldest and most consequential bullrings in Spain, white stucco
        and ochre mouldings with its own name cut into the lintel, and a museum
        tracing the corrida from the eighteenth century onward. Some travellers
        will decline the interior on principle and the façade is worth five
        minutes regardless. For those who go in, the visit is guided or
        audio-guided rather than free-roaming, which is the practical fact that
        catches people out: you join a cycle rather than walk in.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 45 minutes to an hour, guided ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Torre del Oro and the river ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> not usually necessary, but note the hours: daily
        09:30–19:30, extended to 09:30–21:00 in July and August, and
        <strong>09:30–15:00 on days when there is a bullfight</strong>, with the
        ticket office closing thirty minutes before the building ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> the subject is not for you. Longer stay.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="ceramica" level={3}>8. Centro Cerámica Triana</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A small museum in the shell of an old tile works, standing on the kilns
        that fired it, and the single most efficient explanation of why the rest
        of Seville looks the way it does. Triana made the glazed tile that covers
        the city's walls, benches, bridges, church towers and shop fronts; one of
        the rooms is a wall of salvaged trade signs — chemists, hostels, a Singer
        sewing-machine agent, a pre-war coach company advertising a six-day
        excursion to Córdoba, Seville and Granada in French. Half an hour, and
        everything you have already seen reads differently afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 30 to 45 minutes · <strong>Pairs with</strong>
        Mercado de Triana, two minutes away, whose produce stalls trade Monday to
        Saturday roughly 09:00–14:30 and are closed Sundays ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none; a modest admission ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have no interest in how things are made.
        Longer stay, but promoted to first visit for anyone who liked the Alcázar
        tilework.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="palaces"
      number="III."
      name="The Private Palaces"
      epithet="Three aristocratic houses within a kilometre of each other, and the case for visiting only one"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville's nobility built courtyard houses that borrowed Mudéjar
        plasterwork, Italian Renaissance proportion and Roman sculpture and
        somehow reconciled all three. Three are open. They are genuinely different
        from one another and not different enough that a first-time visitor should
        see more than one — a second becomes a comparison and a third a chore. So
        what follows is a choice rather than a list.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="pilatos" level={3} rule={false}>9. Casa de Pilatos — the one to choose</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The finest of the three, and the right answer if you are only visiting
        one. A sixteenth-century house built by an owner who had travelled to
        Italy and the Holy Land and came home determined to reconcile what he had
        seen: Roman busts set into the arches of the courtyard above azulejo
        dados, honeycomb Mudéjar ceilings, a marble fountain with dolphins under a
        Janus head, and rooms named after the stations of the Via Dolorosa. It is
        open every day, which among the ticketed sights in Seville makes it
        unusually forgiving of a Sunday or a Monday.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour, or 90 minutes with the upper floor ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Setas, ten minutes north, and Santa Cruz,
        ten minutes south-west · <strong>Booking</strong> none; open every day
        09:00–18:00; €12 for the ground floor and garden, €6 more for the upper
        floor, which is shown on a guided visit ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you are choosing one of the other two instead.
        First visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/casa-de-pilatos-patio-principal.jpg`}
      alt="The principal courtyard of Casa de Pilatos in Seville: two storeys of arcading with intricate Mudéjar plasterwork and a Gothic tracery balustrade above, blue and white azulejo dados, Roman busts set in roundels, a standing Roman female statue on a plinth, and a marble fountain with dolphins beneath a bearded Janus head on chequered marble paving."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Roman sculpture, Mudéjar plaster and Gothic tracery in one courtyard, and none of it looks borrowed. If you visit one private palace in Seville, this is the one."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="duenas" level={3}>10. Palacio de las Dueñas</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The Seville house of the Alba family, and the one that still feels
        inhabited rather than curated — courtyards of clipped box and blue-tiled
        fountains, family photographs, and the room where the poet Antonio Machado
        was born in 1875. Choose this over Casa de Pilatos if you would rather see
        how a great family actually lived than how one man assembled a
        collection; the architecture is less remarkable and the atmosphere is
        considerably warmer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Alameda de
        Hércules and the northern quarters · <strong>Booking</strong> none for
        the standard visit; open daily 10:00–20:00 April to September and
        10:00–18:00 October to March; €12, reduced €10. A longer guided visit at
        €16 has run on Thursdays and Saturdays at midday, which is worth
        confirming rather than assuming ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you are visiting Casa de Pilatos. Longer stay.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="lebrija" level={3}>11. Palacio de la Condesa de Lebrija</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The strangest of the three and the least visited. In the 1900s the
        Countess of Lebrija had Roman mosaic floors lifted from the ruins at
        Itálica, north of the city, and laid in her own house — so you walk on
        second-century Roman pavement in a private drawing room, under a
        honeycomb ceiling rescued from a demolished medieval palace. The ten-day
        chronicle on this site calls it not famous enough, and that is right. It
        is also the one of the three that most rewards a visitor who has already
        been to Itálica.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 45 minutes to an hour · <strong>Pairs with</strong>
        Calle Sierpes and the Centro shopping streets, on whose edge it stands ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none; open daily 10:00–18:00 September to June
        and 10:00–15:00 in July and August; €12 ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> Roman archaeology leaves you cold. Longer stay.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="parks"
      number="IV."
      name="Parks and Open Ground"
      epithet="The two free things in the south of the city, and the one hour of the day to take them in"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville built itself a park and a ceremonial square for the
        Ibero-American Exposition of 1929 and then kept them. Both are free, both
        are unenclosed, both are in the south, and both are ruined by the middle
        of the day — which makes them the easiest pair of decisions on this page.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="plaza-espana" level={3} rule={false}>12. Plaza de España</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A brick semicircle nearly two hundred metres across, wrapped around a
        canal, crossed by four tiled bridges, its inner wall broken into alcoves —
        one for each Spanish province, each with a painted map and a tiled bench.
        It is the most theatrical thing in the city and it costs nothing. It is
        also unshaded from end to end, which is the whole of the practical
        advice: before about half past nine it is close to empty and lit from the
        side; by noon it is the hottest and most crowded ground on any Seville
        itinerary with nowhere at all to stand out of the light.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 30 minutes to an hour, walking the arc rather than
        the middle · <strong>Pairs with</strong> Parque de María Luisa, directly
        behind it · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Free and ungated. The city
        council has discussed enclosing it and charging non-residents; nothing has
        been implemented and no price has been set ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> never. First visit.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="maria-luisa" level={3}>13. Parque de María Luisa and the Pabellón Mudéjar</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The park begins immediately behind Plaza de España and is the correct
        antidote to it: Moorish fountains, tiled benches, ponds, and trees old
        enough and large enough to make a genuine difference to the temperature.
        At its southern end the Plaza de América holds three Exposition
        pavilions around a pool, of which the Pabellón Mudéjar — now the Museum
        of Arts and Popular Customs — is the finest, and reflects into its own
        lily pond with almost nobody looking at it. The white pigeons on that
        square are said to descend from a delegation's gift to the 1929
        Exposition; whether or not that is true, they behave as though they own
        the place.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour, or 90 minutes as far as Plaza de América ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Plaza de España, which it should always
        follow · <strong>Booking</strong> none. The park is free; the pavilion
        museums charge a small admission ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day. First visit at two days or
        more.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="art"
      number="V."
      name="Art, Tile and What the Convents Left"
      epithet="A golden age of painting, most of it seized from religious houses, and one statue that still stops traffic"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Seville produced Velázquez, Murillo and Zurbarán, and then in the
        nineteenth century the state dissolved the religious houses that had
        commissioned most of what they painted and gathered the results into a
        museum. That history explains both entries here — one where the paintings
        ended up, and one where the devotion they were made for is still going on.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="bellas-artes" level={3} rule={false}>14. Museo de Bellas Artes</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        One of Spain's great collections, housed in a former Mercedarian convent,
        almost always uncrowded, and free to EU citizens. The building does half
        the work: the old convent church survives as a gallery, so a single
        Zurbarán crucifixion hangs alone on a partition wall beneath a frescoed
        dome, and the effect is closer to how the picture was meant to be met than
        any modern hang could manage. The Murillos are the collection's
        reputation; the Zurbaráns are the reason to come.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 90 minutes to two hours ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Plaza de Armas and the northern edge of El
        Arenal · <strong>Booking</strong> none; €1.50 and
        <strong>free to accredited EU citizens</strong>; Tuesday to Saturday
        09:00–21:00 and Sunday and holidays 09:00–15:00 from September to July,
        reduced to Tuesday to Sunday 09:00–15:00 through August;
        <strong>closed Mondays</strong> ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have three days or fewer — it deserves a
        morning and would cost you the river. Longer stay.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/zurbaran-crucifixion-bellas-artes.jpg`}
      alt="A single large Zurbarán crucifixion in a gilded frame hung alone on a pale partition wall in the Museo de Bellas Artes in Seville, beneath the frescoed dome and painted pendentives of the former convent church, with three visitors photographing it from below."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="One painting, one wall, and a convent dome above it. The Museo de Bellas Artes is free to EU citizens and is very nearly always this empty."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="macarena" level={3}>15. Basílica de la Macarena</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        In the north of the city, the Esperanza Macarena stands above her altar
        with painted tears on her face, and a brotherhood four centuries old
        carries her through Seville in the small hours of Good Friday. The
        basilica itself is free and takes fifteen minutes; the museum beside it,
        which holds the processional floats and the embroidered mantles, is what
        makes the trip north worthwhile, because it shows the sheer industrial
        scale of the devotion. Nothing else on this page explains Semana Santa to
        someone who has not seen it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 15 minutes for the basilica, an hour with the museum ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Alameda de Hércules and the surviving
        stretch of Almohad city wall · <strong>Booking</strong> none; the
        basilica is free and the museum around €5, reduced €3. Open Monday to
        Saturday 09:00–14:00 and 17:00–21:00, Sundays and holidays from 09:30 —
        note the long afternoon closure ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> the religious life of the city is not what you
        came for. Longer stay.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="after-dark"
      number="VI."
      name="Seville After Dark"
      epithet="The two hours that cannot be fitted into a daytime plan, and should not be"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="flamenco" level={3} rule={false}>16. Flamenco at a tablao</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Flamenco is not a sight and does not belong in a walking route, which is
        why itineraries leave it out and why a list like this has to put it back.
        It comes in three forms and the distinction matters more than the venue. A
        <strong>tablao</strong> is a room with a stage, a short fixed programme, a
        drink rather than a meal, and performers who are working rather than
        entertaining — choose this. A <strong>dinner show</strong> attaches the
        same art to a set menu and weakens both. A <strong>museum
        performance</strong>, given in a historic patio, sits between them and is
        easiest to book at short notice.
      </p>
      <p>
        What you are watching is not a dance in the ordinary sense. It is an
        argument conducted with feet and hands and a voice about the cost of
        feeling, and it is not designed to charm. An hour of it at close range is
        one of the four things on this page a visitor is likely to still be
        thinking about a year later.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour, plus getting there ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> a late dinner afterwards, since Seville eats
        from about nine · <strong>Booking</strong> yes, a few days ahead for
        anything good; no venue is named here because the one attended on the
        stay behind this page is not identified in the record, and a
        recommendation nobody can stand behind is worth nothing ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> never, unless you have already seen it done
        well. First visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/flamenco-on-a-dark-stage.jpg`}
      alt="Six flamenco performers on a dark stage lit in blue and red: a guitarist in shadow at the left, a woman standing in a red and gold fringed dress with her hand on her hip, three seated figures around a small table, and a second guitarist playing at the right."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="A tablao stage, most of the room in darkness. The economy of it is part of the form — six people, two guitars, four chairs and a table."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The other evening hour belongs to <strong>the Setas de Sevilla</strong> in
        Plaza de la Encarnación — a vast timber lattice the city argued about for a
        decade and has since absorbed into its own vocabulary, carrying a walkway
        across its upper surface, with the old town and the Giralda laid out below.
        Beneath it the <strong>Antiquarium</strong> preserves the Roman and Almohad
        city found when the foundations were dug, which is a neat summary of how
        Seville is put together. Aim for dusk; from April to October a light
        installation runs across the canopy late in the evening. Admission from
        €16, and the published hours run to one in the morning, which is easy to
        misread — confirm them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Seville — Choosing Between Them" detail="16 entries · verified 29 July 2026" detailIcon="🎟">
      <PracticalSection label="What genuinely needs advance booking" level={3} id="booking">
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Real Alcázar</strong> — timed entry, sells out weeks ahead in
          season. The one booking that can spoil a trip if left late.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Cathedral and Giralda</strong> — timed, capacity-limited, and a
          euro cheaper online than at the window.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Flamenco</strong> — a few days ahead for anything worth
          attending.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Everything else on this page</strong> can be bought on the day
          or costs nothing.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="What pairs with what, geographically" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Inside 250 m of Plaza del Triunfo</strong> — Alcázar, Cathedral
          and Giralda, Archivo, Santa Cruz. Four entries, almost no walking.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Along the river, one bridge</strong> — Mercado de Triana and
          Centro Cerámica Triana on the west bank; Torre del Oro and the
          Maestranza on the east; Calle Betis for the evening.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>The southern axis</strong> — Plaza de España then Parque de
          María Luisa, in that order, starting early.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>The northern old city</strong> — Casa de Pilatos, the Setas and
          the Antiquarium, Palacio de las Dueñas, the Alameda, and the Macarena
          furthest out.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Off the grid</strong> — Museo de Bellas Artes, which sits west
          near Plaza de Armas and pairs with nothing else neatly. Give it its own
          morning or leave it.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Closing days to plan around" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Mondays:</strong> Archivo General de Indias and Museo de Bellas
          Artes both close. Casa de Pilatos, the Alcázar and the Cathedral do not.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Sundays:</strong> Mercado de Triana's produce stalls do not
          trade, and the Cathedral opens to visitors only in the afternoon outside
          July and August.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Bullfight days:</strong> the Maestranza closes to visitors at
          15:00.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>August:</strong> the Museo de Bellas Artes drops to a
          morning-only schedule, and the Condesa de Lebrija closes at 15:00.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <PullQuote>
      A list of sixteen things is only useful if it is willing to tell you which
      four matter and which three you may leave out. Otherwise it is an
      inventory, and the city already has one of those.
    </PullQuote>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="omitted">What did not make the list, and who it would still suit</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Six things were considered and left out, and each has a reader it would
        suit better than something above.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hospital de los Venerables</strong> — a fine baroque foundation
        with an exceptional church, five minutes from the Cathedral, left out
        because at the same walking distance the Archivo is free and stranger.
        <strong>Real Fábrica de Tabacos</strong>, the vast eighteenth-century
        tobacco factory now part of the University, with the discovery of tobacco
        carved over its doorway: free, worth ten minutes in passing, not worth a
        detour. <strong>Isla Mágica</strong>, the theme park on the Expo '92
        grounds across the river — genuinely enjoyable and thoroughly peculiar in
        its framing, and the right answer for anyone travelling with children, as
        the ten-day chronicle admits without embarrassment.
        <strong>Convento de Santa Paula</strong>, an active Carmelite community
        selling marmalade through a hatch, which belongs to eating in the city
        rather than to monuments. And <strong>the Cathedral rooftop tours</strong>,
        excellent, and not to be booked in the same year the belfry works are
        still under way.
      </p>
      <p>
        One further omission is deliberate and structural: no day trip appears on
        this page. Córdoba, Cádiz, Itálica and the rest are experiences, but they
        are not experiences in Seville, and the decision about which of them is
        worth a whole day is
        <a href="/best-day-trips-from-seville/">made separately</a>. The same goes
        for the table: eating in Seville is not an attraction to be slotted between
        two monuments, and
        <a href="/seville-tapas-food-guide/">it has its own guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="post-closing">
    <div class="narrative prose" style="max-width:820px; margin:0 auto;">
      <p>
        Sixteen entries is more than most visits will use, and that is the
        intention. Four are effectively compulsory. Three are among the quietest
        important places in the city and will be nearly empty when you arrive.
        Three are variations on one idea, of which you should pick one. And
        several exist here mainly so that a reader with four days rather than two
        knows what the extra two days are for.
      </p>
      <p>
        The thing worth carrying away is smaller than the list. Almost everything
        above is better at nine in the morning or after six in the evening than in
        between, and the middle of a Seville day belongs to a table and a stretch
        of shade rather than to a queue. Arrange the sixteen around that and the
        city stops feeling like a set of tickets. The routes that do the arranging
        are <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">the three-day version</a> and
        <a href="/one-day-in-seville/">the single day</a>; the rest of Andalucía
        is gathered in <a href="/chronicles/">the archive</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

